    2000: f"The 2000s saw the most dramatic price trajectory in the dataset. Driven by surging Chinese demand and constrained supply, prices rose from ~$25 in 2000 to an all-time peak of ${max_price:.2f} in {max_date.strftime('%B %Y')}. The subsequent crash during the Global Financial Crisis brought prices down to ~$41 by December 2008.",
    2010: f"The 2010s were defined by the U.S. shale revolution. Prices remained above $90 through mid-2014 before OPEC's decision not to cut production triggered a crash to under $30 by early 2016. Prices partially recovered but never sustained above $80 for long.",
